On examining the first relation, it appears, on one hand, that the
Constitution is to be founded on the assent and ratification of the people of America, given by deputies elected for the special purpose;
but, on the other, that this assent and ratification is to be given by the people, not as individuals composing one entire nation, but as composing the distinct and independent States to which they respectively belong. It is to be the assent and ratification of the several States, derived from

the operation of these powers, it is national, not federal; in the extent of them, again, it is federal, not national;
and, finally, in the authoritative mode of introducing amendments, it is neither wholly federal nor wholly national.

